From: Joan Karadimov <joan.karadimov@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Do not require qt-sudo on Windows

Each of the four tools refuses to start unless it can find qt-sudo, which
escalates a GUI to root. MSYS2 has no root: pacman writes to an installation
the invoking user already owns, which is why MSYS2 ships no sudo and its
installer sets no special permissions on the install folder. So there is
nothing to escalate to, and qt-sudo is an AUR package that does not and
should not exist here.

Not for upstream: correct only where the privileged path is not needed.
diff --git a/cachecleaner/main.cpp b/cachecleaner/main.cpp
index 61906b5..6af0f80 100644
--- a/cachecleaner/main.cpp
+++ b/cachecleaner/main.cpp
@@ -75,11 +75,16 @@ int main( int argc, char *argv[] )
   }
 #endif
 
+#ifndef Q_OS_WIN
+  // qt-sudo escalates a GUI to root. MSYS2 has no root -- pacman writes to an
+  // installation the invoking user owns -- so there is nothing to escalate to
+  // and no such program to find.
   if (UnixCommand::findExecutable(ctn_QTSUDO).isEmpty())
   {
     qDebug() << "Aborting cache-cleaner as 'qt-sudo' binary could not be found! [" << ctn_QTSUDO << "]";
     return (-5);
   }
+#endif
 
   if (!UnixCommand::isOctoToolRunning(QStringLiteral("octopi-cachecleaner")))
   {
